  "configuration_id": "config_004",
  "reason": "Evidence: Only one experiment is complete (config_001: RNA-only, single profile, PCA-2000 → acc 0.485, F1 0.464), establishing a low baseline. The largest source of uncertainty is whether the RNA representation itself is the bottleneck—PCA-2000 is a linear projection, while scvi_n200 is a learned nonlinear latent representation specifically designed for single-cell data. Question: Does replacing the linear PCA encoder with the learned scvi encoder (holding profile setting, modality, aggregation, and fusion all identical to the baseline) meaningfully improve validation accuracy? Why this is the best next experiment: It changes exactly one design variable (RNA encoder) relative to the established baseline, making the comparison clean and interpretable. If scvi substantially outperforms PCA, it confirms that representation quality is a primary lever and justifies further investment in encoder choice and downstream multimodal designs built on scvi. If it does not, it signals that the RNA signal alone is limited, redirecting budget toward image or multimodal experiments. Under a tight budget, isolating the encoder effect before layering on multiprofile aggregation or image fusion avoids confounding multiple changes and yields the highest information-per-experiment."
}

